    story: `Uwaymir ibn Malik al-Khazraji, known by his kunyah Abu ad-Darda, was among the Ansar of Madinah, though unlike many of his tribesmen he did not accept Islam immediately upon the Prophet's arrival in the city, remaining attached for a period to the idol he had worshipped before, even as his own wife, Umm ad-Darda, embraced the new faith ahead of him. His eventual acceptance of Islam, arrived at after his wife's example and his own subsequent reflection, is remembered in the biographical sources as a conversion reached through careful personal reasoning rather than sudden impulse, consistent with the thoughtful and deliberate temperament that would characterize the rest of his life.

Once he accepted Islam, he devoted himself to it with an intensity that at times required moderation from the Prophet himself. It is reported that Salman al-Farisi, visiting Abu ad-Darda's household, found that he had given himself so completely to fasting and night prayer that he had neglected his wife's rightful due of his time and attention, and Salman reported this to the Prophet, who summoned Abu ad-Darda and told him plainly: "Your Lord has a right upon you, your soul has a right upon you, and your family has a right upon you — so give each their due." (Sahih al-Bukhari 1968) Abu ad-Darda took this counsel to heart, and he became known afterward for a carefully balanced philosophy of religious life, teaching that worldly work undertaken with sincerity was not opposed to devotion but could complement it, provided one's ultimate priorities remained rightly ordered — a nuanced position he is reported to have summarized by saying that he fasted and he broke his fast, he prayed at night and he slept, and he engaged in trade for his family's sake, seeking in all of it only the pleasure of Allah.

He fought at the Battle of Uhud and subsequent engagements, but he became known above all, in the years following the Prophet's death, as one of the foremost judges and teachers of the Levant, sent alongside Mu'adh ibn Jabal and Ubadah ibn as-Samit to instruct the growing population of new converts in Syria. Umar ibn al-Khattab, recognizing his reputation for sound judgment and gentle wisdom, appointed him as judge of Damascus, a position he held with a style remembered for patience and careful reasoning rather than severity, often preferring to guide disputing parties toward reconciliation over strict adjudication wherever the matter allowed for it.

His sayings on the nature of worldly attachment and the urgency of preparing for the Hereafter were widely quoted by later generations of scholars, reflecting a mind given to sustained reflection on the deeper purposes underlying religious practice rather than mere outward performance. He is reported to have remarked to those around him that he loved poverty out of humility before Allah and feared the trial that could come with wealth, and to have counseled his students that the intelligent believer ought to reserve time for reckoning with his own soul just as he reserved time for his worldly affairs, treating the two as inseparable rather than competing obligations.

He remained a central figure of learning and judicial authority in Damascus until his death there in 32 AH, mourned as one of the wisest and most balanced teachers to have emerged from the generation of the Ansar, a man whose own late and carefully reasoned path to Islam had, in the end, produced one of the most measured and thoughtful voices the early Muslim community would come to rely upon.`,
